1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
On today's episode, I sat down with Walt Smith, an investor and thought leader in the Bitcoin and crypto space with experience at Galaxy Digital, Cyberfund, and Standard Crypto.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Walt and I discussed the current state of venture activity around Bitcoin and what is exciting for Bitcoin looking forward.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Without further ado, I hope you enjoy our conversation.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Matt Walsh and Nick Carter are partners at Castle Island Ventures.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
All of these expressed by them or the guests on this podcast are solely their opinions and do not reflect the opinions of Castle Island Ventures.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Guests and hosts may maintain positions in the assets discussed in this podcast.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
You should not treat any opinion expressed by anyone on this podcast as a specific inducement to make a particular investment or follow a particular strategy, but only as an expression of their personal opinion.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
This podcast is for informational purposes only.

9
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Freddie Mac, the two mortgage giants that have been threatened by the housing crisis.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,000
The Bank of England has pumped 75 billion pounds more into Britain's ailing economy with a new round of quantitative easing.

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
You print a couple trillion dollars and all of a sudden people start to worry.

12
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
So out of this worry, we have something called the Bitcoin. Bitcoin.

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Walt, thanks for coming on the pod today. I want to kick off. How did you get into Bitcoin and crypto?

14
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Well, thanks for having me on as well.

15
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:35,000
I was in college and thinking about going to med school and had been reading autobiographies and biographies of doctors and stumbled my way into a econ 101 class with this guy, Jeffrey Herbner, at Grove City College and didn't really know much about economics.

16
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
But I'd heard classic public school or charter school history course that the Great Depression ended World War II.

17
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:55,000
And he started laughing at me and said, do you think bombing half of Europe and forcing all the Americans troops over and killing a ton of the population and using all the producer goods to build tanks that would never be useful again was what ended the Great Depression.

18
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
And I realized I was an idiot.

19
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Fast forward, I ended up majoring in econ. That guy was my advisor.

20
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
He was actually supposed to be Ron Paul's Treasury Secretary had Ron Paul won the 2008 election when he ran in through the school.

21
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
And through the school, I got introduced to Austrian econ. The school is the first to never take federal funding.

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:18,000
So it's very libertarian and it has a very small but vocal Bitcoin minority on campus.

23
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
A ton of people in the space went to the school.

24
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
And from there, I leapfrogged into the startup world and realized medicine is very bureaucratic.

25
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
The world's very bureaucratic and startups are fighting the good fight against that.

26
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
And Bitcoin's leaving the charge in terms of monetary startups.

27
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:37,000
So you like the lack of bureaucracy in the individual agency of Bitcoin?

28
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Yeah, I think the idea that you don't need the Federal Reserve to provide a put on your savings count or your 401k or a put on the S&P 500.

29
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
And we don't need to structure the world to have this index mindset where everyone has exposure to this equity in the U.S.

30
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,000
But people are gate-kept from it outside of it.

31
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:03,000
And the U.S. benefits from pumping out its dollars into the world and then having the most valuable tech companies or companies in the S&P 500.

32
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:11,000
I think Bitcoin is really this idea that you can radically control your savings and your net worth and personal accountability and sovereignty.

33
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Which economist or which economic school of thought do you think is most aligned with Bitcoin then?

34
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
That's a good question. I've never been asked that.

35
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:27,000
I would say the Austrian school, if you go back to the Bitcoin Doc forums, is by far the most quoted from guys like Ron Paul who are politicians to the fathers of the school.

36
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Like Karl Manger back in Austria.

37
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Ludwig von Mises in Austria who fled to Switzerland and then ended up at NYU.

38
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Hans Sennholz who taught at Grove City College.

39
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Of course, you have the more radical ones.

40
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Bastiat.

41
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Murray Rothbard who is friends with my undergrad advisor.

42
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
But I would say either the classical school in the Chicago school is still pretty libertarian.

43
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
So I think they're like open to the idea.

44
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:54,000
They do have the money equation which says inflation needs to happen.

45
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Otherwise, we'll have a credit crisis.

46
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
I'm not a huge fan of that outlook.

47
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
That's a good question. I can't really think of another school of thought that's more pro Bitcoin.

48
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Yeah, I thought it was very high eccentric in terms of having non-sovereign money.

49
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
I was having this conversation with someone at one point.

50
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:19,000
It seems to me like even beyond Bitcoin, we'll hopefully have this escape of sovereign currencies being the canonical store of value.

51
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
That's my hope for on-chain finance more broadly.

52
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Is that I could have money in an index of US housing and that's one form of currency.

53
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
And I could have money in index of natural gas and that's one form of money.

54
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Versus this reliance to your point on state issued money.

55
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Hayek was very much a student of Ludovic Mises who was a student of Karl Manger.

56
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:48,000
And this idea of the theory of money is what is the good that has the features or the application that has the features that is going to make it the best form of money.

57
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
And everyone starts using it and there's a bit of a network effect there.

58
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,000
You'll have exposure to housing.

59
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:04,000
If a housing producer, if someone comes up with tiny homes and now we have super cheap housing in America, thank God, then you'll have exposure just by holding the most scarce, the most secure thing.

60
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
I think that's what Bitcoin is really getting for.

61
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:09,000
I agree with you generally, but I throw Hayek in there with the Austrians.

62
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
I think the hardcore Austrians would say he was a little bit of a turncoat, but I don't care enough to label him as something else.

63
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
The denationalization of money.

64
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:22,000
That's the book I'm thinking of, which I think is worth a read for any crypto centric person.

65
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:23,000
It's a good rec.

66
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Yeah, put it on your list.

67
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Speaking of Bitcoin and speaking of let's call it emerging new currencies, what do you think is investible or can be new in and around that?

68
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:41,000
I think proof of work and proof of stake are the bedrock of understanding any network, but I'm open to there being other simple designs.

69
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
I don't love the top down, UBI, centrally planned outlook.

70
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
I think Sam Altman has his one.

71
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Elon has tweeted about getting UBI.

72
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,000
I think it'd make a lot of sense.

73
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:56,000
He has a little bit of a what's the line from Larry David bite store.

74
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
He's spiteful of who Sam Altman is, so he might copy him a little bit.

75
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Do you see universal basic income and proof of stake or proof of work blockchains as being interrelated?

76
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Yeah, I think the idea is UBI is like let's have central issuance versus proof of stake is like let's have some form of distributed issuance and proof of work is like let's have totally scarce supply and figure out the security later on.

77
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:24,000
I think there could be like a proof of personhood version of UBI where you uniquely prove your personhood.

78
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:31,000
I think WorldCoin tried to do this and some other people have as well, but there's really big issues with how do you verify is the hardware tamperable?

79
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
How do you re-verify the person so they can't spread the currency around?

80
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,000
So I'm open to there being alternatives to Bitcoin, but what I found is a lot of networks are still test nets for Bitcoin.

81
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
We're going to have EVMs on Bitcoin.

82
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,000
I think within 10 years we'll have Solana tethered to Bitcoin in some form or fashion.

83
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Whether it's a fork or not doesn't really matter.

84
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:54,000
So I think ultimately it has the right properties to eat everything else in the world, at least in the cryptosphere.

85
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:02,000
I do worry about how does it buttress against the issues that gold had where gold was easy to seize or like easy to stop at the point of sale because it's very hard to carry.

86
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:07,000
And I think Bitcoin could run into that in a world where it's mass adopted in more of its own 401Ks.

87
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:08,000
Can you actually spend it?

88
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:09,000
Does that matter?

89
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:15,000
So to rewind a little bit, and let's go piece by piece there, the newest use cases of cryptocurrencies,

90
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:22,000
which I think of as being a lot of these financial apps that might let you send money across the world.

91
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:23,000
There's been a stablecoin boom.

92
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:29,000
The questions they ask of on-chain finance are, is it fast and is it cheap to move money around?

93
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:38,000
And for better or for worse, there's not a lot of consideration of the design of these on-chain systems at the moment, I would say, from a lot of these new builders.

94
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:44,000
And you start to get more federated blockchain systems like a Canton network.

95
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:54,000
How important do you think people should think of the settlement systems like a proof of work governance system, proof of stake for an on-chain system versus not,

96
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:01,000
especially when we're looking at the emergence of more currencies and also where Bitcoin will move in the future?

97
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
I think it's incredibly important.

98
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Why? I think this is an important point for us to touch on.

99
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:12,000
Well, I think the clearest difference you could paint is a permission validator set versus a permissionless one.

100
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:15,000
And whether that's entry or exiting the validator set.

101
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:22,000
And I think if you have a permission set that it's not fluid, it's less synonymous and people can't exit the network.

102
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:26,000
And at the same time, those validators are an easier choke point for governments.

103
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,000
It's an easier choke point for them to censor.

104
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:31,000
We saw the OFAC stuff of 2022 on Ethereum.

105
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:41,000
So I think if you have permissionless entry and exit and over time running a node or mining is cheaper or it's more open or it's the set of participants that are doing it.

106
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
If it's 100 participants, but they're all going through one pool, it's not great.

107
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,000
But if you have 100 and they each have their own pool or they have some accountable pool,

108
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:55,000
then I think the users have really good guarantees and there's really good competition for including user transactions.

109
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:00,000
And if you have a form of geographic decentralization, then governments are going to have a harder time top down.

110
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:07,000
I think the Ethereum meme is World War III, but I think Bitcoin has energy as the input and energy is fully distributed.

111
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:12,000
So this is Bitcoin 101, but explain the risks of a centralized validator set.

112
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
I think it could be like who's making the chips.

113
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
I mean, we've all seen this China, Taiwan thing forever.

114
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Spectre over people, especially around April.

115
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:24,000
It could be from the manufacturer, which doesn't have to be the same as the actual chip fab.

116
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
It could be at the pool level.

117
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:32,000
But if you have a consortium that's really strong, it's a lot easier for the government to just show up and be like, oh, tell this consortium what to do.

118
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
We saw this with Libra. Libra got shut down in Congress.

119
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
I don't think you could shut down Bitcoin in Congress.

120
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
China tried to, and the network kept running.

121
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
People still had their money and people could still move their Bitcoin around.

122
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,000
Like in Venezuela and fleeing in 2020 during the elections or like around then.

123
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:50,000
Didn't have to worry about the Chinese miners getting shut down.

124
00:09:50,000 --> 00:10:04,000
I do also see a certain world, though, because I think that the powerful financial institutions will be incentivized to create these federated chains or financial networks where assets transact on their chains.

125
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:14,000
So one outcome I could see here is they accumulate as much Bitcoin as they can, essentially put it in cold storage and then mint essentially claims on a Bitcoin.

126
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:19,000
It's almost like a stable coin where a claim on a Bitcoin can move across their blockchain.

127
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
Do you see something like that as a functional risk?

128
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
I think we've had this.

129
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:26,000
I think it's different, but we have CBBTC.

130
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:27,000
We've had RatBitcoin.

131
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
The majority of the world has projected that.

132
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:39,000
And I think if you look at really early Bitcoin amps, things like Lava, things like Odinfun, there's significant traction, even though there are early versions or early prototypes.

133
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
I think those founders are doing a job, but I don't think so.

134
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:46,000
I think the people who are buying it in the 401k are not going to use it to begin with.

135
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
I think they're just long term supply, which is great.

136
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:54,000
And maybe they'll start thinking about the rest of their portfolio differently when they hold Bitcoin in their 401k for 10 years and it goes up.

137
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
But the actual users, I think, have projected the custodial model.

138
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:04,000
I don't think they'll care who and what financial giant or like fintech giant is orchestrating the network.

139
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:15,000
So then in that vein, another belief some people hold is that you'll get this equitization of Bitcoin and that eventually people will own Bitcoin through these equity wrappers, through ETFs, and that will spread more broadly.

140
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
Do you reject that over the longer term?

141
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:23,000
I want to be an empiricist here, where anyone who's rejected this for the last five years has been totally wrong.

142
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
Thanks to Mark Taylor.

143
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:29,000
I think BitMEX put this out a couple of weeks ago, so the number is likely higher.

144
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:35,000
But Vanguard, which famously hates Bitcoin and crypto, owns 8% of strategies, like 8% of the stock.

145
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
And it's like, OK, if you don't believe in the equitization of Bitcoin, sure.

146
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:44,000
But if you look at the traditional financial plumbing, we have all these passive buyers of MicroStrategy and S&P 600.

147
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
And if they get into the 500, we're going to have even more.

148
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
And that's just issuance that Sailor will have.

149
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
The stock is very special.

150
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:52,000
It's at Liberty ETFs.

151
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:56,000
It has a bunch of advantages that no treasury code could really copy intentionally.

152
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
I think it just fell into Sailor's lap.

153
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
I don't think he even knew what was going to happen.

154
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
But yeah, I think we're seeing the equitization of Bitcoin.

155
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:07,000
I don't think we can say it's not going to happen when MicroStrategy has 3% of this fly.

156
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:15,000
It potentially opens up new business models for how to approach all this idle Bitcoin then, because you have all these passive holders.

157
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Do you believe that?

158
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:17,000
Yeah, I do.

159
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:25,000
I was going to ask you as well, do you think there's a risk-free rate to Bitcoin or is there presumably a way to earn a yield or long-term interest on your Bitcoin?

160
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:26,000
So begs that question.

161
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:31,000
But in theory, you'll have all this Bitcoin that is then just sitting somewhere.

162
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Not to be too much of an appearance.

163
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:36,000
I think you can look at Ethereum and be like, where does the yield come from?

164
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:37,000
A lot of it's issuance.

165
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
There's some execution fees.

166
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:40,000
There's some congestion fees.

167
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
There's native staking rewards, though.

168
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:43,000
Yeah, issuance.

169
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:49,000
So I think if you look at that, then you can be like, what would the rate of Ethereum staking be without issuance?

170
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:54,000
If it was just execution or if it was just pure lending, and it's not a great lending rate.

171
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,000
And I think you see this in the Bitcoin OTC lending market.

172
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:58,000
It can be as high as 14%.

173
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
You can get better terms if you make a permission list.

174
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
You open the set of lenders to the world and more retail dollars can come in.

175
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,000
Sure.

176
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:10,000
I think that's what a lot of the private credit people are hoping for by putting private credit on chain.

177
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:15,000
But I think the risk free rate is not really risk free because you're taking on US dollar risk.

178
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:16,000
So I think it's like a misnomer.

179
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:19,000
So I would just say there will be a benchmark rate.

180
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:24,000
It will be like the Bitcoin lending rate that will provide all the other rates with something to go off of.

181
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
What about funding rates for perps?

182
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:27,000
That's an interesting question.

183
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:34,000
I think if you're asking if you could architect Athena with Bitcoin and get a stable coin out of it, I would just say no.

184
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
Because there's no stake either.

185
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
No, but I think people are trying to build Bitcoin funds around this idea.

186
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
Yeah, the market has some really good products.

187
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:48,000
You have CME futures and you have Bitcoin perps on Binance, and you can get basis trading and cash and carry trading.

188
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:49,000
That's healed.

189
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,000
Yeah, I'm less convinced it'll be something that's durable there.

190
00:13:52,000 --> 00:14:01,000
Because I think with the 401k's and Fidelity and everyone buying Bitcoin in their retirement accounts, it's going to semi-stabilize.

191
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
BitMEX also has this other good research post on their historical funding rates.

192
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:09,000
And every year, the gaps between the extremes of funding get narrower and narrower.

193
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:14,000
If you go back and look at 2019 or 2022 to today, the funding rate slowly collapses.

194
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,000
I don't know how to think about the funding rate relative to the lending rate.

195
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:18,000
I think that's an interesting question.

196
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:21,000
So you think that trade goes away over time, essentially?

197
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:27,000
Yeah, or it's risk that people are comfortable taking because you need access to an exchange of some sort.

198
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:28,000
That makes sense.

199
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:33,000
What do you think of innovation around Bitcoin in the last year or so and looking forward to the next year?

200
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:40,000
I think if you're a cryptographer, it's probably been extremely interesting seeing the guys at Alpen Labs, the guys at Citria,

201
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:46,000
the guys working on like BitMEX or like Rod and Linus from ZeroSync, which led the charge initially.

202
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
Jeremy Rubin's also done some stuff at Char.

203
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:54,000
There's a ton of guys really pushing the edge on what they think they can do in terms of ZK optimization.

204
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:58,000
Outside of that, I don't think there's been a huge amount of stuff going on.

205
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
And end users don't see that yet.

206
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
The hope is that they will in a couple years' time.

207
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:03,000
Yeah.

208
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:10,000
And I think if you're a cryptographer or very passionate about Bitcoin having L2s that can provide more scale, that's self-custodial.

209
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:11,000
It's been terrific.

210
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:17,000
I think companies like Lightspark and Arc have been doing different approaches that look a little bit more like traditional lightning channels.

211
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:21,000
And there seems to be a lot of momentum there, but nothing has hit the users yet.

212
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:24,000
So it's pretty hard to judge anything without cohort data from users.

213
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:30,000
And in your view, is the biggest end goal they're making to Bitcoin for payments?

214
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
Is it similar to the lightning view?

215
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
Yeah, I think we've talked about this before.

216
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:41,000
But I think if Bitcoin doesn't get a velocity where people can actually spend it in size and use it around,

217
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:47,000
it could run into the same problems gold had in the 1900s where starting with FDR and ending with Richard Nixon,

218
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:56,000
we had progressively higher and higher restrictions on gold, eventually ending in private investment in gold being illegal essentially until 1974.

219
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:06,000
And that made dollarization and inflation and exporting debt to enable the government to bring in a lot of welfare programs and waste a lot of private resources possible.

220
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:10,000
And I think Bitcoin wants to help people get the dollar out of their life.

221
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
And to do that, you need velocity.

222
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:22,000
It's interesting, though, because the Bitcoin ETF, as we both know, was one of the most successful ETF launches ever, if not the most successful in terms of a product.

223
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:31,000
And the demand that came in from that was not at all contingent on Bitcoin's use as a payment method or having velocity.

224
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
None of that Bitcoin is used for that use case.

225
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
And we haven't seen that use case take off.

226
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:44,000
So therefore, I would conclude that the demand we've seen for the Bitcoin ETF has not been because Bitcoin can be functionally used as a payment method right now.

227
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
I love this question.

228
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:53,000
It goes back to like, why did it matter that the Medici's were clipping gold off of gold corn in 1400?

229
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
The idea is fundamentally that inflation is a silent killer.

230
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:59,000
People here are like, oh, inflation is 2 percent.

231
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:02,000
And now after COVID, maybe it's 3 percent median worldwide.

232
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:10,000
That's half of your money in 24 years, and that's 75 percent of your money's value over 50 years, which is probably the average career length.

233
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,000
So your earnings are actually 0.24 percent of what they should have been.

234
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:16,000
But people don't think about this in those terms.

235
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:23,000
They think about the here and now and live pretty low demand elasticity or time preference, or they're just too busy to think about it or like track it.

236
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:30,000
And I think if gold was really easy to spend and still had its same properties, then people would probably just use gold.

237
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:33,000
It'd be a lot harder to get people to use dollars.

238
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:40,000
And then if people are using dollars, there's this whole very complex treasury market where you can inflate and people aren't even really aware of it.

239
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:45,000
And then the official measures given by Big Brother in the sky are always tampered with.

240
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:49,000
And there's no really universal basket to actually track inflation.

241
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:55,000
I always tell my sister when she asked me about this stuff is the official measure since COVID is, I think, 34 percent or something.

242
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:58,000
So if you make 100K, you need to make 134K.

243
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:03,000
But if you look at the McChicken at McDonald's, it went from a dollar to two dollars and seventy four cents.

244
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
So I'm like, which one is it?

245
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:14,000
The McChicken index. So I like this grounding in inflation or like comparing it to what a theoretical rate of inflation actually is, or even just the idea that people don't like inflation.

246
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:25,000
With that in mind, do you think the spending use case is just I'm Walt and I would rather hold most of my money in Bitcoin and therefore I want to buy my McChicken using Bitcoin.

247
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:30,000
Is that why the velocity and the spending is important or is it for some other reason?

248
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
I think there is a mix of reasons.

249
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:42,000
I think you get like a compounding beneficial effect where if your medium of exchange is also your savings unit and your payment unit, then the value of the thing itself goes higher.

250
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:49,000
I think if people have gold and they were buying with gold bullions, they would have some reserve balance of gold in their wallet.

251
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:53,000
People have savings account. They have like their emergency fund. That's all in cash and it's all getting into the way.

252
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:59,000
I think that would be more like reserve demand for Bitcoin, but I think it would also create more point of sales demand.

253
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:06,000
And if it was fast enough, you would be benchmarking your dollar spending to spending something that doesn't have inflation.

254
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:11,000
And I think the price stability would also get better. So there's a lot of these things would compound together.

255
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:15,000
That makes sense. No, it would improve the landscape in multiple ways.

256
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
Do you use Bitcoin for payments?

257
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:30,000
No, I do not. I have at certain moments, but I think there's this issue with the payment operating system where people have tried to use Lightning there for years and it's been really hard to get restaurants or grocery stores.

258
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:38,000
I would say I think of Bitcoin as like my ultimate savings account. I don't own a lot of cash and I never will. But no, I think the friction is there for me.

259
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:44,000
Do you think we get the Bitcoinization of any nation state in the foreseeable future?

260
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:50,000
That's a great question. I think the network state stuff feels like it needs Bitcoin to succeed in order to succeed.

261
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:51,000
Why?

262
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:59,000
I think if you're just starting a new nation, you can have a mix of well off people and not as well off people and they just build a new country.

263
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:02,000
We've seen that happen hundreds of times in history.

264
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:11,000
But if you're like, we're going to intentionally create something that is competitive with San Francisco and it's going to be in Tijuana in Mexico, and we're going to like induce interstate competition.

265
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:16,000
Those people are used to pretty high quality of life. They're used to having a job at a good startup that's well funded.

266
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:23,000
And that's the idea. How do we fix America? By like injecting this competition between states and that's special economic zones.

267
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:26,000
That's network states down in Honduras.

268
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:36,000
And I think to do that, you need a capital asset that is really valuable so that the private investment can actually scale and have a really good user experience for being a participant in the network state.

269
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:44,000
So then I think without having a really ideologically aligned, strong, denationalized money, it doesn't really work.

270
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:49,000
But yeah, I think we're starting to see it. There's one in Costa Rica. There's the one in El Salvador.

271
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:52,000
I know guys already who like completely live on a Bitcoin standard.

272
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:56,000
Interesting. Are they going to stay doing that, do you think, or is that temporary?

273
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:12,000
No, I think 100% they will. I think if you return to tradition and reject modernity and are off the grid and surf a lot and the thing you pay for is always going up in value and you live on a beach in Costa Rico, I don't see a reason you'd come back to society.

274
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:16,000
I buy that. I think you've pretty much fully subscribed at that point.

275
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:24,000
What do you look at as the biggest challenge or challenges for Bitcoin? And is the security budget a lead issue in your mind or what stands out to you?

276
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:35,000
I view Bitcoin as the most capitalist chain. Bitcoin is never going to add features where if you build a protocol or a company or a payments thing on top that it's going to eat you someday because it's pretty ossified.

277
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:42,000
Even getting pretty simple things like CTV or like OPCAT, shout out our investment tapper wizards fighting the good fight for OPCAT.

278
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:48,000
It just takes an insane amount of political willpower to push those things through, which is ultimately a good thing.

279
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:56,000
But I think the danger I do see is that we don't get velocity, that we don't get payments. I don't think it's like 401k balances.

280
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:05,000
I think that's ultimately a good thing that people own some Bitcoin and start to think about why does my Bitcoin keep going up in value and why does my house and dollars keep getting more expensive?

281
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:14,000
But I think if we don't get payments, I think the traditional financial specter is always going to try and extract and insert itself. And maybe it's paper Bitcoin that's fake.

282
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:19,000
I know like the laser-eyed folks are pretty nervous about that. I don't think it's the security budget.

283
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:32,000
I think because it's so unexpressive, we'll always be able to add things, intact things on that will secure it, whether it's a finality gadget for proof of work, whether it's people up in the mountains who are heating their homes with ASICs.

284
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:41,000
There's a bunch of startups working on that. I think it's a really awesome idea seeing key as a beneficial byproduct, not as a bad thing. So yeah, I'm not worried about the security budget.

285
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:45,000
Do you think stable coins or other on-chain assets are threatening to Bitcoin?

286
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:53,000
You know, this one I struggle with. I feel like I've thought about this since I used to trade at Galaxy and would talk to traders.

287
00:22:53,000 --> 00:23:04,000
I think right now, if you're an import exporter in Nigeria or a guy with a savings account in Venezuela, getting access to the dollar is awesome and Bitcoin is probably too volatile for you.

288
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:10,000
I think if Bitcoin is less volatile and let's say it's worth 20 trillion or whatever in market capital, I'm not saying it's going to happen.

289
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:17,000
But in this theoretical world, I do think it would be an alternative to the dollar. It will lose some people to the dollar.

290
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:30,000
Ultimately, though, I think we really don't know how this is going to fall because we haven't seen nation states tackle the stable coin thing with their own legislation, which is to say we've seen Middle Easterns try and fight the petrodollar.

291
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:37,000
We've seen in the 90s, the Federal Reserve and also European central banks were pretty nervous about the euro dollar market.

292
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:40,000
Stable coins are big and they really benefit the U.S.

293
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:49,000
We have not seen a government say, oh, we're actually going to put the same capital controls that Japan has used for its entire history to stop its citizens from using the dollar.

294
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:54,000
I think until you know that, you don't really know how to think of it versus Bitcoin at scale.

295
00:23:54,000 --> 00:24:06,000
Yeah, I'm generally compelled to think if the entire world were on stable coins as opposed to fiat money, it would be beneficial to Bitcoin and therefore it should be beneficial to Bitcoin in theory.

296
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:14,000
But my counter argument is I think it does threaten the monetary status from an opportunity cost perspective.

297
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:17,000
Sort of like you said, I think it's the Ethereum and the altcoins.

298
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:27,000
Like I think stable coins on Ethereum have directly or indirectly harmed ETH's price value because people just carry that asset more now or they speculate using it.

299
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:30,000
Yeah, I think Tron is also a really good example.

300
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:34,000
People are now TRX's money, but like a terrific amount of stable coins.

301
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
Yeah, it's a great example.

302
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:37,000
So I would echo that.

303
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:49,000
I think if you're also looking for like one world government, one of the best things you could do is be like, let's let everyone use a stable coin that's tied to the US and we'll have Tether be like the biggest treasury buyer in the world.

304
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:58,000
So implicitly the citizens of the world are fostering the US government from becoming even more Machiavellian and this Leviathan that nothing can slay.

305
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:04,000
So I want to circle back, especially as we start to close out here, Bitcoin for payments.

306
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:07,000
You and Jack Dorsey maybe can jam over that one.

307
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:11,000
What gets us closer to that or what happens structurally with the Bitcoin market?

308
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:18,000
And is there anything else that you think needs to happen or people should be caring about when it comes to Bitcoin?

309
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:22,000
Yeah, I'm not going to be like we're at the end of history for designs.

310
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:30,000
But I think if you look at altcoins on Ethereum, if you look at base, a lot of people have built payments infrastructure around base because it's cheap enough.

311
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:39,000
And I think if we get a form of non-custodial, if you have a full on forced exit hatch that works from a Bitcoin altitude down to Bitcoin,

312
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:46,000
whether that's using a new ZK verification opcode, whether that's using some form of a BitVM or what people are calling Glock now,

313
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:50,000
whether that's using something that looks like Kavion, Tapper Wizards, does it work on?

314
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:56,000
I don't think it matters. I think it's just if you have low enough payments, people will start to build the payments infrastructure.

315
00:25:56,000 --> 00:26:02,000
And then I think over time, it'll evolve to like a more fractured network where you have L3s on top of L2s,

316
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:06,000
you have multiple L2s that are connected, and that'll allow geographic payments,

317
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:13,000
almost looking like a traditional database architecture where you co-locate things where they need to be, but they can still connect.

318
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:20,000
It's the old idea of sharding in like a traditional database, just where it's permissionless and non-custodial. That feels like what we need.

319
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:24,000
Well, Waltz, we'll leave it there. It's been a pleasure. Thanks for coming on.

320
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
Thanks for having me, Wyatt.

